12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"March 6–8, 1862","place":"Gosport Navy Yard, the Elizabeth River, Newport News Point, and Hampton Roads, Virginia","result":"CSS Virginia surprised the United States blockading squadron on March 8. It rammed and sank USS Cumberland, forced USS Congress to surrender and then burned it, and fired at the grounded USS Minnesota before withdrawing because of darkness and the falling tide. More than 250 United States sailors were killed. Virginia lost two men killed, and its commander Franklin Buchanan was wounded. USS Monitor arrived that night","facts":[{"label":"Virginia departed Gosport","value":"About 11 a.m., March 8, 1862"},{"label":"Opening shot","value":"About 1:20 p.m. from the armed tug USS Zouave"},{"label":"Virginia opened fire","value":"About 2 p.m."},{"label":"Cumberland rammed","value":"About 3 p.m."},{"label":"Congress surrendered","value":"About 4:30 p.m."},{"label":"Virginia withdrew","value":"About 6:30 p.m."},{"label":"United States ships destroyed","value":"USS Cumberland and USS Congress"},{"label":"United States sailors killed","value":"More than 250, according to the episode"},{"label":"Virginia casualties stated in the episode","value":"2 killed; several wounded, including Franklin Buchanan"}],"maps":[{"title":"Hampton Roads today","caption":"The action occurred where the James and Elizabeth rivers enter Hampton Roads. Virginia came from Gosport Navy Yard on the Elizabeth River. Cumberland and Congress were off Newport News Point; the other large United States warships were nearer Fort Monroe.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-76.48%2C36.76%2C-76.20%2C37.05&layer=mapnik&marker=36.987%2C-76.309"},{"title":"The March 8 attack","caption":"Virginia passed Congress, rammed Cumberland, turned back to force Congress to surrender, fired from long range at Minnesota, and then withdrew toward Sewell’s Point. The diagram numbers the sequence described in the episode.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html","image":"/images/hampton-roads-march-8-battle-map.svg","alt":"Tactical map of CSS Virginia’s March 8 attack showing Cumberland sunk, Congress surrendered and burned, Minnesota grounded, and Virginia withdrawing"},{"title":"Virginia’s route from Gosport","caption":"This wider diagram shows the ten-mile approach down the Elizabeth River, the positions off Newport News and Fort Monroe, and Monitor’s arrival from the Chesapeake later that night.","source":"https://monitor.noaa.gov/150th/hampton.html","image":"/images/hampton-roads-march-8-map.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Hampton Roads showing CSS Virginia moving from Gosport Navy Yard toward USS Cumberland and USS Congress off Newport News"},{"title":"Hampton Roads in 1862","caption":"The U.S. Army reconnaissance map shows the channels, shorelines, forts, and military positions around the roadstead. Virginia’s 22-foot draft restricted it to the deeper channels.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html","image":"/images/hampton-roads-1862-map.jpg","alt":"1862 U.S. Army reconnaissance map of the Hampton Roads vicinity with military positions"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:07","audioTime":67,"title":"The episode covers the first day at Hampton Roads","detail":"The March 8 fighting pitted CSS Virginia against the wooden ships of the United States blockading squadron. The ironclad duel happened the next day."},{"time":"2:00","audioTime":120,"title":"Virginia was still incomplete","detail":"Franklin Buchanan reached Gosport in late February. Workers were still finishing the ship, its powder supply was short, and the crew was incomplete."},{"time":"2:37","audioTime":157,"title":"Army volunteers filled the crew","detail":"Lieutenant John Taylor Wood obtained permission to recruit nearby Confederate soldiers. He selected 80 artillerymen and former sailors from 200 volunteers."},{"time":"3:25","audioTime":205,"title":"Deep draft restricted Buchanan’s options","detail":"Virginia drew nearly 22 feet of water. Shallows and narrow channels limited where it could maneuver in the Elizabeth River and Hampton Roads."},{"time":"4:45","audioTime":285,"title":"The first night attack was canceled","detail":"Buchanan planned to leave on March 6 and surprise the ships at Newport News, but weather, unfinished work, and the pilots’ refusal to navigate at night delayed the sortie."},{"time":"5:31","audioTime":331,"title":"The casemate received a coating of tallow","detail":"Catesby ap Roger Jones asked for time to coat the armor with animal fat in the hope that projectiles would glance away more easily."},{"time":"7:00","audioTime":420,"title":"The last powder arrived on March 7","detail":"Virginia received enough gunpowder the day before the attack."},{"time":"7:05","audioTime":425,"title":"Mallory proposed attacks beyond Hampton Roads","detail":"Stephen Mallory suggested that Virginia attack New York after breaking the blockade. The ship’s draft and poor seakeeping made such plans impractical."},{"time":"9:05","audioTime":545,"title":"Virginia left Gosport at 11 a.m.","detail":"Workers jumped to the dock as the ship moved away. Gun-port shields and internal compartments were unfinished, and the guns and engines had not been tested under operating conditions."},{"time":"10:53","audioTime":653,"title":"The approach took about ninety minutes","detail":"The navy yard was about ten miles from Hampton Roads. Virginia made five or six knots and needed tugs to help keep its bow pointed downriver."},{"time":"11:48","audioTime":708,"title":"Buchanan selected Cumberland as the first target","detail":"He believed Cumberland’s rifled stern gun posed the greatest danger and told the engineer that he intended to ram the ship."},{"time":"12:03","audioTime":723,"title":"The crews sighted each other near Sewell’s Point","detail":"Around 12:30 p.m., Virginia entered Hampton Roads and its crew saw Cumberland and Congress off Newport News."},{"time":"12:29","audioTime":749,"title":"More than sixty United States vessels were present","detail":"The roadstead held warships, transports, supply ships, tugs, tenders, and dispatch vessels. All had wooden hulls."},{"time":"13:09","audioTime":789,"title":"The senior squadron commander was absent","detail":"Louis Goldsborough was at Hatteras Inlet. Captain John Marston of USS Roanoke was the senior naval officer in Hampton Roads, but Roanoke’s propulsion machinery was under repair."},{"time":"14:16","audioTime":856,"title":"The main ships were divided","detail":"Minnesota, Roanoke, and St. Lawrence lay near Fort Monroe. Congress and Cumberland were stationed several miles west off Newport News Point."},{"time":"14:29","audioTime":869,"title":"Congress and Cumberland were vulnerable","detail":"Congress was a 50-gun sailing frigate with about 400 men. Cumberland was a 24-gun sailing sloop under executive officer George U. Morris."},{"time":"15:46","audioTime":946,"title":"The army warning did not reach the squadron","detail":"Signalers at Newport News reported smoke on the Elizabeth River, but the army command did not promptly share the warning with naval officers."},{"time":"16:31","audioTime":991,"title":"Zouave fired the opening shots","detail":"The armed tug investigated the smoke and fired six rounds from its 30-pounder Parrott rifle at about 1:20 p.m."},{"time":"19:46","audioTime":1186,"title":"Buchanan ordered the crew to battle stations","detail":"He told the crew the sortie was an attack and directed the ship to eliminate Cumberland and Congress before the ships near Fort Monroe could intervene."},{"time":"20:44","audioTime":1244,"title":"Virginia opened fire around 2 p.m.","detail":"The bow Brooke rifle fired at Cumberland from less than a mile. Congress answered as the range closed, but its solid shot glanced from the casemate."},{"time":"21:16","audioTime":1276,"title":"Virginia’s first broadsides hit Congress","detail":"Grape and shell killed or wounded gun crews, disabled guns, and started fires. Congress cut its anchor cables and was towed into shallow water."},{"time":"23:38","audioTime":1418,"title":"Virginia rammed Cumberland","detail":"At about 3 p.m., the iron ram struck Cumberland amidships and opened a large hole below the waterline. The ram broke off as Virginia backed away."},{"time":"25:45","audioTime":1545,"title":"Cumberland sank while firing","detail":"The crew continued firing as the ship flooded. Two Virginia crewmen were killed at a bow gun port, and many wounded sailors aboard Cumberland drowned below deck."},{"time":"27:12","audioTime":1632,"title":"Virginia required about thirty minutes to turn","detail":"After sinking Cumberland, the slow ironclad made a wide turn. Confederate gunboats from the James River joined the action."},{"time":"27:55","audioTime":1675,"title":"Minnesota and St. Lawrence grounded","detail":"The large United States steam frigates attempted to move west and ran into shallow water. St. Lawrence was freed; Minnesota remained aground."},{"time":"28:12","audioTime":1692,"title":"Virginia returned to Congress","detail":"At about 4 p.m., the ironclad raked the grounded frigate from roughly 150 yards. Lieutenant Joseph B. Smith was killed."},{"time":"28:51","audioTime":1731,"title":"Congress surrendered","detail":"Commander William B. Smith raised white flags after about thirty minutes of renewed fire. Confederate boats and tugs moved in to accept the surrender and remove prisoners."},{"time":"29:22","audioTime":1762,"title":"Shore fire interrupted the surrender","detail":"United States artillery and musket fire struck the Confederate boats. Buchanan ordered heated shot fired into Congress."},{"time":"29:49","audioTime":1789,"title":"Buchanan was wounded","detail":"A rifle bullet struck his thigh while he was exposed on the upper deck. Catesby ap Roger Jones took command of Virginia."},{"time":"30:31","audioTime":1831,"title":"Jones fired at Minnesota from long range","detail":"Virginia could not approach the grounded frigate because of shallow water. Darkness and the ebbing tide made continued action unsafe."},{"time":"31:03","audioTime":1863,"title":"Virginia withdrew at about 6:30 p.m.","detail":"Jones took the ship toward Sewell’s Point and anchored under Confederate shore batteries around 8 p.m."},{"time":"31:28","audioTime":1888,"title":"The attack destroyed two warships","detail":"Cumberland had sunk and Congress burned until its magazine exploded after midnight. The episode states that more than 250 United States sailors were killed."},{"time":"32:08","audioTime":1928,"title":"Monitor arrived before Virginia returned","detail":"The new United States ironclad reached the roadstead, reported to Roanoke, and moved to protect Minnesota before the March 9 battle."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Louis M. Goldsborough","command":"North Atlantic Blockading Squadron","subordinates":"Absent at Hatteras Inlet during the March 8 attack"},{"commander":"John Marston","command":"Senior naval officer at Hampton Roads and captain of USS Roanoke","subordinates":"Roanoke, Minnesota, St. Lawrence, Congress, Cumberland, and supporting vessels"},{"commander":"George U. Morris","command":"USS Cumberland","subordinates":"Executive officer commanding the sloop in the captain’s absence"},{"commander":"Joseph B. Smith","command":"USS Congress","subordinates":"Lieutenant commanding until killed; former captain William B. Smith then resumed command"},{"commander":"John E. Wool","command":"Department of Virginia at Fort Monroe","subordinates":"United States Army troops and shore batteries around Newport News and Fort Monroe"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Stephen R. Mallory","command":"Secretary of the Navy","subordinates":"Set strategic objectives for Virginia"},{"commander":"Franklin Buchanan","command":"CSS Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the attack until wounded; Catesby ap Roger Jones then took command"},{"commander":"John Randolph Tucker","command":"James River Squadron","subordinates":"Patrick Henry, Jamestown, and Teaser supported Virginia"},{"commander":"Charles C. Simms","command":"Virginia’s bow pivot gun","subordinates":"Fired the ironclad’s opening shot at Cumberland"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Franklin Buchanan","role":"Planned and commanded Virginia’s attacks on Cumberland and Congress until wounded","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Catesby ap Roger Jones","role":"Executive officer who took command of Virginia after Buchanan was wounded","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"George U. Morris","role":"Commanded Cumberland and continued fighting until the ship sank","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph B. Smith","role":"Commanded Congress during most of the action and was killed before its surrender","side":"United States"},{"name":"William B. Smith","role":"Former captain still aboard Congress who resumed command and surrendered the disabled ship","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Taylor Wood","role":"Confederate lieutenant who recruited soldiers to complete Virginia’s crew","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"H. Ashton Ramsay","role":"Virginia’s chief engineer who kept its unreliable machinery operating","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Marston","role":"Senior United States naval officer present in Hampton Roads during the surprise attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"McKean Buchanan","role":"Paymaster aboard Congress and brother of Virginia’s commander; escaped the burning ship","side":"United States"},{"name":"Thomas O. Selfridge Jr.","role":"Officer aboard Cumberland who sent Zouave to investigate Virginia’s approach","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Joseph B. Smith","side":"United States","role":"Commanding officer of USS Congress","outcome":"Killed by Virginia’s fire during the renewed attack on the grounded frigate."},{"name":"Franklin Buchanan","side":"Confederacy","role":"Commanding officer of CSS Virginia","outcome":"Wounded in the thigh by rifle fire while directing the attack on Congress. Catesby ap Roger Jones assumed command."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/virginia-destroys-congress-painting.jpg","alt":"Painting of CSS Virginia attacking and burning USS Congress","caption":"Xanthus Smith’s wartime painting shows Virginia attacking Congress. The frigate surrendered, was set on fire, and exploded after midnight.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"image":"/images/css-virginia-drydock.jpg","alt":"CSS Virginia in dry dock at Gosport Navy Yard in about February 1862","caption":"Virginia in dry dock shortly before the battle. It entered combat without a full trial and with some construction unfinished.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"image":"/images/hampton-roads-1862-map.jpg","alt":"1862 U.S. Army reconnaissance map of Hampton Roads","caption":"A wartime reconnaissance map of the harbor, channels, and surrounding military positions.","source":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 107","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/107-clash-of-the-ironclads-part-the-third"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: Hampton Roads","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/browse-by-topic/wars-conflicts-and-operations/civil-war/cw-operations-and-engagements/1862-civil-war/hampton-roads.html"},{"label":"Naval History and Heritage Command: March 8 at Hampton Roads","url":"https://www.history.navy.mil/our-collections/photography/wars-and-events/the-american-civil-war--1861-1865/css-virginia-destroys-uss-cumberland-and-uss-congress--8-march-1.html"},{"label":"NOAA Monitor National Marine Sanctuary: Battle of Hampton Roads","url":"https://monitor.noaa.gov/150th/hampton.html"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Hampton Roads","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va008"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of the Ironclads","url":"https://home.nps.gov/articles/battle-of-the-ironclads.htm"}]}}]}]
